<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Perqed</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Perqed is winding down." />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <style>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light dark;
        --bg: #ffffff;
        --fg: #16161a;
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --bg: #131316;
          --fg: #e8e8e6;
        }
      }
      html {
        -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 6rem 1.5rem;
        background: var(--bg);
        color: var(--fg);
        font: 400 1.0625rem/1.7 Georgia, "Iowan Old Style", "Times New Roman", serif;
      }
      main {
        max-width: 33rem;
        margin: 0 auto;
      }
      h1 {
        margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
        font-size: 1rem;
        font-weight: 600;
      }
      p {
        margin: 0;
      }
      @media (max-width: 30rem) {
        body {
          padding: 3.5rem 1.25rem;
        }
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <h1>Perqed</h1>
      <p>
        We had a lot of fun building this, but ultimately apart from the simulated
        annealing result which seems to have been a real result early on, we could not
        get this to work well enough to keep pace ahead of just asking large models to
        solve problems. We'll repurpose the URL for something else math related soon.
      </p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
